1
00:00:03,438 --> 00:00:07,528
3 January 2009.

2
00:00:07,728 --> 00:00:13,978
The first Bitcoin block is mined, and an ordinary laptop is enough to win the reward.

3
00:00:17,166 --> 00:00:23,536
Seventeen years later, the network makes about a thousand quintillion (10²¹) guesses every second.

4
00:00:23,736 --> 00:00:29,890
Laptops gave way to graphics cards, and then to warehouses of purpose-built chips.

5
00:00:33,078 --> 00:00:40,360
For a small miner at home, the chance of winning a single block is now about 1 in 16,000 a year.

6
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:45,946
And yet, solo miners still won 22 blocks in 2025.

7
00:00:49,134 --> 00:00:54,256
Every block also carries a quiet fingerprint of whoever built it:

8
00:00:54,456 --> 00:00:59,074
which header bits their machines roll, and how they fill the block.

9
00:01:02,262 --> 00:01:08,080
We mined all 975,000 blocks to ask three questions.

10
00:01:08,280 --> 00:01:11,914
Can behaviour alone reveal who mined a block?

11
00:01:12,114 --> 00:01:15,772
Does the data rediscover the eras of mining?

12
00:01:15,972 --> 00:01:21,958
And should a miner in Hong Kong join a pool, gamble solo, or switch off?

13
00:01:25,146 --> 00:01:27,652
This is HashAtlas.
